WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.331
Speaker 1: It's great to be joined today by
Nick Lane, who's professor of evolutionary

00:00:04.331 --> 00:00:09.620
biochemistry at University College London,
also the author of five books, some of which

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:14.839
I've read, including Transformer The Deep
Chemistry of Life and Death.

00:00:14.839 --> 00:00:16.600
Really appreciate you being on today.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:17.600
Thank you.

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:18.670
Speaker 4: Well, thank you for the invitation.

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:19.710
Pleasure to be here.

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:24.480
Speaker 1: So I think maybe to to kind of
frame the conversation.

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:29.269
Certain areas of science are studied in particular
ways.

00:00:29.269 --> 00:00:36.980
Biology, for example, has increasingly looked
at genetic information as one particular way

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:44.610
that we study the origins of life, intelligent
life, consciousness, whatever the the approach

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:49.350
to that study has been a particular way over
the last several decades.

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:55.090
Do you think, generally speaking, that in
these fields science is focusing on the right

00:00:55.090 --> 00:00:58.050
questions and the right approaches?

00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:02.050
Or is there sort of like a paradigm shift
that you think would be more useful in answering

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:04.960
some of the key questions we don't yet have
answers to?

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:08.630
Speaker 4: Well, I do think a paradigm shift
would be useful.

00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:13.290
That's not to say everything that people have
been studying is wrong.

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:17.670
You know, it's it's taken us a long way down
the path.

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:23.220
But the danger is that if you think it's only
about genes and information and so on, then

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:28.300
you kind of miss the broader point that there's
more to life than just genetic information.

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:35.770
It's kind of a statement of the obvious, but
but it's amazing how kind of how much tunnel

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:40.470
vision you can have in research that people
know effectively where the funding is going

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:43.550
to come from, the kind of things that will
attract funding, the kind of things that you

00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:44.550
can do.

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:49.101
A lot of science is about what you can physically
go and do in the lab, what you know, what

00:01:49.101 --> 00:01:53.370
technical equipment do you have to go and
explore this question?

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:57.930
And then I have to say, science is intrinsically
quite conservative.

00:01:57.930 --> 00:02:04.670
And any ideas that seem to be too far outside
the box tend to be squashed.

00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:07.190
You know, and there's lots of ideas outside
the box.

00:02:07.190 --> 00:02:12.040
And probably a fairly high proportion of them,
probably including some of my own, ought to

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:13.040
be squashed.

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:15.000
You know, you don't squash them without thinking
about it.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:21.160
You squash them after after, you know, testing
them, trying it a little bit just to see where

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:22.160
it takes you.

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:27.020
Speaker 1: Well, that's very interesting because
there are some in the science communication

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:32.430
community, I guess we would call it, for lack
of a better term, who often repeat and like

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:38.340
to say that one of the great differences between
science and religion is that within organized

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:43.590
religion, dissenting views are often squashed
or dismissed.

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:48.640
You become a pariah of sorts, in contrast
to the scientific world where actually being

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:56.700
able to debunk or disprove previously held
beliefs is greatly revered and celebrated

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:57.700
and welcomed.

00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:01.670
And there's great celebrity available to scientists
if they're able to do that.

00:03:01.670 --> 00:03:04.450
Is that only true in some sense or in some
cases?

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:08.790
Speaker 4: There is a lot of truth in that,
which is to say I won't speak for the for

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:11.020
the religious side of it, but for the scientific
side.

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:16.750
Yes, I think most scientists most of the time
set out to try and do something new, to try

00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:20.470
and, you know, overturn the applecart to some
extent.

00:03:20.470 --> 00:03:27.030
But the whole system of peer review, the whole
system of kind of double checking everything

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:32.189
and making sure that this is bona fide, that
this is solid research, that you've not missed

00:03:32.189 --> 00:03:36.040
something obvious, that you've you've got
all the controls and all this stuff.

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:37.959
Well, who does the peer review?

00:03:37.959 --> 00:03:42.180
The people who do the peer review or the people
who have, in effect, already established their

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:45.120
careers within establishing the current paradigm?

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:49.640
And so as a group of people, they are, by
nature, I mean, kind of almost by definition,

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:51.069
relatively conservative.

00:03:51.069 --> 00:03:55.470
And if you really do come up with a completely
new paradigm, then you really will overturn

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:57.500
a lot of people's work.

00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:01.790
And so being revolutionary is not an easy
thing to do.

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:03.040
I'm not saying I'm revolutionary.

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:06.780
I think I'm just talking plain common sense,
to be honest with you.

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:07.780
But.

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:13.019
But but this is this is something that anybody
who is genuinely has transformative ideas

00:04:13.019 --> 00:04:16.509
in science will come up against the conservative
ness of peer review.

00:04:16.509 --> 00:04:21.350
And it's, as I say, probably nine out of ten
people with what look like mad ideas will

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:23.760
really have ideas that will turn out not to
be true.

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:26.440
And it's probably right that they don't get
too far.

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:31.260
But one out of those ten maybe, you know,
genuinely brilliant, a genius who who's it?

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:35.160
And they face kind of problems with the social
construction of science.

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:36.160
Yes.

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:39.919
Speaker 1: To talk a little more specifically
about one of your books, Transformer The Deep

00:04:39.919 --> 00:04:41.650
Chemistry of Life and Death.

00:04:41.650 --> 00:04:44.880
As we kind of mentioned at the start of the
conversation, there's been a lot of focus

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:48.720
on genetic information in the field of biology.

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:53.680
And one of the areas that you explore, again,
to the extent that I hopefully understood

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:58.120
it somewhat correctly in the book, is about
metabolism and.

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:00.020
Looking at sort of the cellular level.

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:04.289
And when we talk about life and death, really
looking at what is it that is happening there?

00:05:04.289 --> 00:05:10.060
Now, we've talked to scientists who specialize
in human longevity, for example, and they've

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:16.330
explained in a way that is relevant to that
particular field the idea of over time, the

00:05:16.330 --> 00:05:22.819
body losing the ability to replace cells of
different types, a deterioration of sorts

00:05:22.819 --> 00:05:26.410
that that takes place, etc., that's specific
to the field of longevity.

00:05:26.410 --> 00:05:29.900
And the language sort of serves that, that
purpose.

00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:35.050
Can you talk a little bit about the angle
that you take here and really this process

00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:36.950
that's at the core that you describe?

00:05:36.950 --> 00:05:43.550
Speaker 4: Yeah, what I'm really interested
in is, is how energy works in biology, because

00:05:43.550 --> 00:05:46.710
it's not it's not as you might imagine, it
is actually electrical.

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:49.510
I suppose a lot of people might immediately
think it's electrical.

00:05:49.510 --> 00:05:54.120
If you think about Frankenstein's monster
or something, you have an electric shock and

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:55.350
off they go.

00:05:55.350 --> 00:05:59.060
And we are all our cells inside ourselves.

00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:00.820
We have these things called mitochondria.

00:06:00.820 --> 00:06:04.631
They're often called the power packs of cells,
and they have a they have an electrical charge

00:06:04.631 --> 00:06:07.789
across their membrane, which is a, you know,
a trivial charge.

00:06:07.789 --> 00:06:09.880
We're talking 150, 200 millivolts.

00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:15.260
But if you are to shrink yourself down to
the size of a molecule and kind of stand next

00:06:15.260 --> 00:06:21.720
to the membrane, the membrane so thin is five
millionths of a millimeter in thickness over

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:26.449
nanometers thick that the actual strength
of the field that you would experience if

00:06:26.449 --> 00:06:30.830
you were there is about 30 million volts per
meter, which is equivalent to a bolt of lightning.

00:06:30.830 --> 00:06:32.930
So this is what's keeping us alive.

00:06:32.930 --> 00:06:36.740
And nobody imagined until a few decades ago.

00:06:36.740 --> 00:06:40.380
This is not new information, but a few decades
ago it came as really quite a shock to discover

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:46.530
that effectively life is powered by electrical
charges and electrical fields across membranes.

00:06:46.530 --> 00:06:49.759
And so for me, the interesting question is
why?

00:06:49.759 --> 00:06:50.850
How did that get going?

00:06:50.850 --> 00:06:52.330
It's quite a complex system.

00:06:52.330 --> 00:06:53.699
What happened at the Origin of Life?

00:06:53.699 --> 00:06:56.310
This is conserved across all of life.

00:06:56.310 --> 00:07:03.069
And also, if we look across the whole 4 billion
year history of life on earth, it's pretty

00:07:03.069 --> 00:07:04.069
weird as well.

00:07:04.069 --> 00:07:08.210
What we actually see is the first 2 billion
years, it's just bacteria and really nothing

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:09.210
else.

00:07:09.210 --> 00:07:13.250
And actually, if you think about it from the
point of view of bacteria that are still there,

00:07:13.250 --> 00:07:14.330
they're still just bacteria.

00:07:14.330 --> 00:07:18.220
And so we have a 4 billion year history of
life on earth, which is just bacteria.

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:23.900
And then at one point, which is to say all
the evidence suggests at one point you you

00:07:23.900 --> 00:07:29.590
have the emergence, what looks like quite
an abrupt emergence in geological terms of

00:07:29.590 --> 00:07:33.680
our own type of cell, what is called eukaryotic
cells, or that means a cell with a nucleus,

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:39.270
and the nucleus is where we put our DNA in
the genes.

00:07:39.270 --> 00:07:44.760
So we have the same cell type that plants
do and, you know, mushrooms, fungi and so

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:49.449
on do and large single celled critters, things
like amoeba.

00:07:49.449 --> 00:07:51.129
They also have the same cell structure.

00:07:51.129 --> 00:07:56.199
Now, if you think if you think about, you
know, we know we've got all of these genes,

00:07:56.199 --> 00:07:59.900
sequences from the history of life, from bacteria
all around the world.

00:07:59.900 --> 00:08:03.910
And what it tells us is that the bacteria
and another group that look a lot like them

00:08:03.910 --> 00:08:10.699
called Archaea, have basically explored the
kind of gene sequence space up all the possibilities

00:08:10.699 --> 00:08:15.889
that you could have across all of evolution
from exploring different gene sequences.

00:08:15.889 --> 00:08:20.639
They've done that much better than other types
of cell have done, eukaryotic cells have,

00:08:20.639 --> 00:08:23.080
and yet somehow they got stuck in this rut.

00:08:23.080 --> 00:08:24.949
They remain simple in their morphology.

00:08:24.949 --> 00:08:28.250
They're not simple in their genetics or their
biochemistry, but they're simple in their

00:08:28.250 --> 00:08:33.450
size and their shapes and, you know, the kind
of things going on inside.

00:08:33.450 --> 00:08:40.180
And so it seems, I would say, that says they're
not limited by genetics, they're not limited

00:08:40.180 --> 00:08:44.180
by the searching of sequence space, they're
limited by something else.

00:08:44.180 --> 00:08:49.589
And that's something I think is the structure,
because what all eukaryotes have in common

00:08:49.589 --> 00:08:55.230
is they they they swallowed a bacterium 2
billion years ago, which went on to become

00:08:55.230 --> 00:09:00.079
these power packs, emoticons with this powerful
electrical charge across all the membranes.

00:09:00.079 --> 00:09:06.620
So effectively they internalized respiration
in a compartment which which allowed them

00:09:06.620 --> 00:09:12.310
to scale up, become large and complex, and
more or less get beyond the constraints that

00:09:12.310 --> 00:09:13.310
energy has.

00:09:13.310 --> 00:09:18.490
So obviously cells need energy, but not many
people have thought about, well, how is it

00:09:18.490 --> 00:09:19.790
provided?

00:09:19.790 --> 00:09:24.070
And what we actually have is we have genes
in our mitochondria and genes in our nucleus.

00:09:24.070 --> 00:09:28.029
You need both sets of genes to control this
electrical charge.

00:09:28.029 --> 00:09:33.760
And that's that's a kind of a shift in cell
structure, which changed the whole history

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:35.620
of evolution and everything that's possible.

00:09:35.620 --> 00:09:39.360
And if we look for life on other planets and
you think, are we going to find the same kind

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:40.360
of things?

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:41.360
Are we going to find people?

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:42.360
Are we going to find intelligent aliens?

00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:45.910
Whatever it may be, the likelihood is we'll
find bacteria for exactly the same reasons

00:09:45.910 --> 00:09:49.279
that the Earth was dominated by bacteria for
most of its history.

00:09:49.279 --> 00:09:54.620
Speaker 1: When we think forward based on
this framework, you know, I recently read

00:09:54.620 --> 00:09:57.529
the book Precipice by Toby Ord.

00:09:57.529 --> 00:10:05.300
Which looks at some of the more, I guess we
would say, anthropogenic risks to humanity.

00:10:05.300 --> 00:10:14.230
And he sort of goes through nuclear war, climate
change, etc., and kind of frames the conversation

00:10:14.230 --> 00:10:19.730
in from what we know, here's roughly how long
species last.

00:10:19.730 --> 00:10:25.800
Here's roughly what we think about mammalian
species and takes sort of that approach to

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:30.260
how long might Homo sapiens last if we were
to not destroy ourselves?

00:10:30.260 --> 00:10:37.529
But it's a very socially informed kind of
perspective when you think about the future

00:10:37.529 --> 00:10:39.339
of Homo sapiens.

00:10:39.339 --> 00:10:45.160
Is that side the one that seems most predictive,
or is what we're talking about here, quite

00:10:45.160 --> 00:10:52.089
literally, how cells get their energy and
the waste of cells, which do you think as

00:10:52.089 --> 00:10:57.790
humanity continues to would presumably advance
technologically, will bear a greater weight

00:10:57.790 --> 00:11:00.959
on what eventually happens to humans?

00:11:00.959 --> 00:11:07.800
Speaker 4: Well, I mean, I, I fear for the
next phase of human history.

00:11:07.800 --> 00:11:15.760
I mean, we are very capable of doing this
up badly and effectively, eliminating ourselves

00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:16.760
from the planet.

00:11:16.760 --> 00:11:20.830
Now, we will not, under any circumstances,
eliminate life from the planet.

00:11:20.830 --> 00:11:24.850
I suppose a full scale nuclear Armageddon
might just do it, but even then there'll be

00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:29.500
plenty of bacteria left, I'm sure, down in
the crust and so on, and probably plenty of

00:11:29.500 --> 00:11:31.080
eukaryotes down there as well.

00:11:31.080 --> 00:11:38.571
So this transition from just a bacteria to
2 to 2 eukaryotic cells, I don't think we'd

00:11:38.571 --> 00:11:39.730
have to go through that again.

00:11:39.730 --> 00:11:46.529
And we've had, you know, major mass extinctions
in the past where whereas 70, 80, 90% of of

00:11:46.529 --> 00:11:54.000
species get wiped out, you move the clock
forward by five, 10 million years and you

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:55.670
have a fully vibrant planet again.

00:11:55.670 --> 00:11:59.209
So really what we're in danger of doing is
destroying ourselves and everything we care

00:11:59.209 --> 00:12:01.930
about.

00:12:01.930 --> 00:12:03.580
And and then the planet will readjust.

00:12:03.580 --> 00:12:08.279
And in 10 million years, it'll just be without
us.

00:12:08.279 --> 00:12:09.279
Would we get back?

00:12:09.279 --> 00:12:11.889
All the majesty that we see around us is hard
to say.

00:12:11.889 --> 00:12:15.050
It depends just how effective we are in wiping
everything else out.

00:12:15.050 --> 00:12:20.000
So that's my biggest fear because there are
tipping points and it's it's all very well

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:24.300
to say we should be optimistic we can fix
any problem we put our minds to.

00:12:24.300 --> 00:12:26.000
I would agree with that.

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:29.350
But we need the political will, we need the
political governance.

00:12:29.350 --> 00:12:34.580
And, you know, we're also capable of scuppering
the best laid plans that humanity can come

00:12:34.580 --> 00:12:35.580
up with.

00:12:35.580 --> 00:12:38.160
And it may be we just leave it too late beyond
beyond some tipping point.

00:12:38.160 --> 00:12:39.930
So that's the biggest threat to humanity.

00:12:39.930 --> 00:12:44.610
It doesn't matter how long mammalian species
might normally last, we can do ourselves in

00:12:44.610 --> 00:12:46.149
within the next 20 years or something.

00:12:46.149 --> 00:12:48.740
Speaker 1: We're in a special, unique situation
now.

00:12:48.740 --> 00:12:53.200
Speaker 4: Really think we really are unique
in the sense that I don't think any other

00:12:53.200 --> 00:12:59.029
species beyond well, not even a virus could
take out the entire planet in the way that

00:12:59.029 --> 00:13:00.380
we're capable of doing.

00:13:00.380 --> 00:13:06.410
So I don't know that it's necessarily helpful
to think in general terms about the future.

00:13:06.410 --> 00:13:08.990
I think we need to fix very specific problems
now.

00:13:08.990 --> 00:13:11.880
But if if we're thinking about, you know,
if we do go into those general terms and think

00:13:11.880 --> 00:13:20.750
about, well, can we, for example, solve lifespans
so that we live indefinitely?

00:13:20.750 --> 00:13:23.860
I think it will be difficult.

00:13:23.860 --> 00:13:27.970
What I mean, there's two there's two aspects
in biology that says it's possible.

00:13:27.970 --> 00:13:34.150
One of them is that life itself is effectively
immortal in the sense that there is an unbroken

00:13:34.150 --> 00:13:38.110
chain of cells that go right back to the origin
of life.

00:13:38.110 --> 00:13:41.600
And those cells were metabolically active
all the way through.

00:13:41.600 --> 00:13:43.310
So so there's no necessity.

00:13:43.310 --> 00:13:46.250
That damage accumulates and overcomes everything.

00:13:46.250 --> 00:13:49.480
And then there are some, you know, things
like flatworms and so on.

00:13:49.480 --> 00:13:54.880
There's a there's a few fairly simple animals
that are, in effect, immortal and can live

00:13:54.880 --> 00:13:57.110
indefinitely, but they're usually pretty simple.

00:13:57.110 --> 00:14:04.100
And the things that we most care about are,
you know, our minds and our minds are incredibly

00:14:04.100 --> 00:14:10.120
delicate structures made up of sign up trillions
of synaptic connections.

00:14:10.120 --> 00:14:14.990
And to preserve those is a is a very difficult
thing to do.

00:14:14.990 --> 00:14:21.320
And the decision evolution is made historically,
if you like, is how much effort do I want

00:14:21.320 --> 00:14:26.300
to put into the preservation of the organism
compared to the replication of the organism?

00:14:26.300 --> 00:14:32.710
And the decision for humans is, well, you
know, 70, 80, 90 years, that allows you time

00:14:32.710 --> 00:14:38.199
to have a few kids and look after them and
even the grandparents can help look after

00:14:38.199 --> 00:14:39.910
them, whatever it may be.

00:14:39.910 --> 00:14:43.190
This is a kind of an evolutionary bargain,
if you like, in the bargain says you have

00:14:43.190 --> 00:14:48.820
a limited lifespan because this is all we're
going to invest in your own prolongation as

00:14:48.820 --> 00:14:49.920
an organism.

00:14:49.920 --> 00:14:55.300
So we need to know the kind of the details
of the small print that says, here's why you

00:14:55.300 --> 00:14:57.050
here's why your life is coming to an end.

00:14:57.050 --> 00:15:00.910
And see if we can fix that.

00:15:00.910 --> 00:15:03.399
A lot of people wouldn't want to fix that.

00:15:03.399 --> 00:15:07.620
I think it would be quite difficult in any
case to do it.

00:15:07.620 --> 00:15:12.620
So it's not it's not if you like, a threat
in the immediate future.

00:15:12.620 --> 00:15:16.279
I don't think, though, you could talk to other
people who think that we already have people

00:15:16.279 --> 00:15:18.550
on this planet who are going to live to a
thousand or something.

00:15:18.550 --> 00:15:19.550
Well.

00:15:19.550 --> 00:15:20.550
Speaker 1: Short.

00:15:20.550 --> 00:15:21.550
Short of that.

00:15:21.550 --> 00:15:22.550
And I've interviewed some of those people.

00:15:22.550 --> 00:15:23.550
Yeah.

00:15:23.550 --> 00:15:26.410
Aubrey de Gray has been saying for 20 years,
I think that the first person to live to 1000

00:15:26.410 --> 00:15:28.740
is live in the limited time we have left.

00:15:28.740 --> 00:15:32.850
It's almost cliche because it's so repeated
now and sometimes things get repeated.

00:15:32.850 --> 00:15:34.980
And do we do they really make sense?

00:15:34.980 --> 00:15:43.959
But there's this idea that the best we can
hope to do with standard human bodies is about

00:15:43.959 --> 00:15:47.360
120 or 125 years, and that's beyond.

00:15:47.360 --> 00:15:49.620
Speaker 4: Maximum human lifespan.

00:15:49.620 --> 00:15:50.620
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:15:50.620 --> 00:15:55.009
And so that generally makes sense to you that
until we really start making modifications

00:15:55.009 --> 00:15:59.279
that make us not quite human, that's going
to be a very difficult upper limit to get

00:15:59.279 --> 00:16:00.279
to get beyond.

00:16:00.279 --> 00:16:02.829
Speaker 4: I think it will be very difficult.

00:16:02.829 --> 00:16:03.829
Yes.

00:16:03.829 --> 00:16:06.319
I'm not saying it's not possible, but.

00:16:06.319 --> 00:16:07.930
But but it will be it will be difficult.

00:16:07.930 --> 00:16:08.930
Yes.

00:16:08.930 --> 00:16:09.990
And I think it'll take us a while longer.

00:16:09.990 --> 00:16:14.300
I don't think we understand biology nearly
well enough yet to get beyond that limit personally.

00:16:14.300 --> 00:16:15.320
Speaker 1: Right.

00:16:15.320 --> 00:16:19.970
We've been speaking with Nick Lane, professor
of Evolutionary Biochemistry at University

00:16:19.970 --> 00:16:20.970
College London.

00:16:20.970 --> 00:16:25.769
We'll link to a number of his books, including
Transformer, The Deep Chemistry of Life and

00:16:25.769 --> 00:16:26.769
Death.

00:16:26.769 --> 00:16:27.769
I really appreciate your time today.

00:16:27.769 --> 00:16:28.769
Speaker 4: Thank you.

00:16:28.769 --> 00:16:28.773
It was a pleasure.

